# KVM / Libvirt Virtualization Lab

## Remote Management (`virt-manager` & `virt-viewer`)

Manage the headless KVM server securely from a Linux workstation over SSH without exposing libvirt TCP ports:

```bash
# Connect virt-manager to the remote hypervisor
virt-manager -c qemu+ssh://<user>@<server-ip>/system

# Open a standalone SPICE console directly to a specific VM
virt-viewer -c qemu+ssh://<user>@<server-ip>/system <vm-name>
```

## Guest Integration (SPICE Clipboard & Display)

* **Linux Guests (Ubuntu / Kali):**
  ```bash
  sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y spice-vdagent
  sudo systemctl enable --now spice-vdagent
  ```
* **Windows 11 Guest:** Install `spice-guest-tools` for VirtIO display scaling and SPICE clipboard integration.

## Isolated Lab Network (`virbr1`)

Define and start the isolated internal lab network so VMs (**Windows 11**, **Kali Linux**, **Ubuntu**) can communicate with each other without exposing lab traffic to the main LAN:

```bash
sudo virsh net-define isolated-lab-net.xml
sudo virsh net-start isolated-lab
sudo virsh net-autostart isolated-lab
```

## Snapshot Workflow for Break/Fix Testing

Before modifying bootloaders (`/etc/fstab`, GRUB), firewall rules, or running security labs:

```bash
# Create a clean snapshot
sudo virsh snapshot-create-as <vm-name> clean-state

# Roll back if needed
sudo virsh snapshot-revert <vm-name> clean-state
```
